    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "Tax-inclusive pricing works, and the acceptance test passes under `bin/e2e`. For the test's client, a $120 line at 20% tax shows Tax mode \"Inclusive\", tax $20.00, and the total stays $120.00.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Setting it:** each client has a \"Prices include tax\" checkbox on both the add and edit forms. The client page shows which way that client is priced.\n- **The maths:** for a tax-inclusive invoice, the sales tax and levy are worked back out of the taxable lines after the discount: price \u00d7 rate / (100 + tax % + levy %). The total stays the discounted subtotal. The \"Taxable amount\" row shows what's left once the taxes are taken out, and the Tax and Levy rows say \"included\".\n- **Existing data:** current clients and invoices default to tax added on top, so no amounts change.\n\n**One design choice to check:** each invoice keeps its own copy of the setting, taken from the client when the invoice is created. If you later change a client's setting, only their draft invoices are recalculated. Sent invoices keep the figures they were issued with. The alternative was to always read the setting live from the client, but then turning it on or off would change the tax shown on invoices already sent while their totals stayed the same.\n\n**What changed**\n- **Database:** a new migration, `V37__tax_inclusive_pricing.sql`, adds a `tax_inclusive` column to `client` and to `invoice`.\n- **Server:** the invoice calculation in `Invoice.java` handles the inclusive case. The client form data and responses carry the new flag; if a client update leaves it out, the current setting is kept.\n- **Test seeding:** I also changed the test seed endpoint (`TestSupportController`), which isn't one of the files I was told not to touch. It now reads `taxInclusive` for clients and stores inclusive invoices with the right total.\n- **Front end:** the invoice page shows the new mode text on `data-testid=\"invoice-tax-mode\"`. The client forms and client page have new `data-testid`s: `client-form-tax-inclusive`, `client-edit-form-tax-inclusive` and `client-tax-mode`. No existing `data-testid` was renamed or removed.\n\nThe task asked for an OfficeFloor server, but this app is plain Spring Boot (only the Java package is named `net.officefloor`), so I followed the Spring conventions in `CLAUDE.md`.",
